    "---\n",
    "title: \"Chapter 19. Embeddings, Retrieval, and RAG\"\n",
    "subtitle: \"Semantic search, nearest neighbors, and retrieval-augmented generation\"\n",
    "format:\n",
    "  html:\n",
    "    code-fold: true\n",
    "    code-tools: true\n",
    "jupyter: python3\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.1 Why retrieval belongs in a mathematical NLP book\n",
    "\n",
    "Large language models estimate conditional distributions over tokens.  In an autoregressive model, the central object is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p_\\theta(w_t \\mid w_{<t}).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This distribution is learned from training data and depends on the information contained in the prompt.  A model may have learned general linguistic and statistical patterns, but it may not know the contents of a new course policy, a recent paper, a private document collection, or a local advising rule.  Retrieval-augmented generation, usually abbreviated as **RAG**, addresses this limitation by adding an explicit search step.\n",
    "\n",
    "A RAG system separates two mathematical operations:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Retrieval:** given a query $q$, choose documents or chunks $c_1,\\dots,c_k$ from a collection $C$.\n",
    "2. **Generation:** produce an answer from a conditional model using both the query and the retrieved context.\n",
    "\n",
    "A simplified RAG model has the form\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p_\\theta(y \\mid q)\n",
    "\\approx\n",
    "p_\\theta(y \\mid q, r_k(q)),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "r_k(q) = (c_{i_1},\\dots,c_{i_k})\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "is the top-$k$ retrieved context.  In a more probabilistic formulation, the retrieved document is treated as a latent variable:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(y \\mid q)\n",
    "= \\sum_{c \\in C} p_\\theta(y \\mid q,c) p_\\eta(c \\mid q).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This chapter studies the mathematics behind $p_\\eta(c \\mid q)$: embeddings, similarity functions, nearest-neighbor search, vector indexes, ranking, and retrieval evaluation.  The emphasis is not on building a production search engine, but on understanding the objects that make semantic retrieval possible.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
    "## Main mathematical theme\n",
    "\n",
    "RAG turns part of language modeling into a problem in finite-dimensional geometry.  Queries and documents are mapped into vectors; retrieval becomes an optimization problem over a point cloud; generation becomes conditional modeling with selected external evidence.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.2 Learning goals\n",
    "\n",
    "After studying this chapter, students should be able to:\n",
    "\n",
    "- define a sentence or document embedding as a map into $R^m$;\n",
    "- compare sparse lexical retrieval and dense semantic retrieval;\n",
    "- compute dot-product, cosine, and Euclidean nearest neighbors;\n",
    "- explain why normalization changes retrieval geometry;\n",
    "- formulate retrieval as a maximum-score optimization problem;\n",
    "- understand approximate nearest-neighbor search at a high level;\n",
    "- explain chunking, indexing, retrieval, reranking, and context construction;\n",
    "- evaluate retrieval using precision, recall, MRR, and nDCG;\n",
    "- describe failure modes of RAG using probability and geometry.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.3 From word embeddings to document embeddings\n",
    "\n",
    "In Chapter 8 we represented individual words using an embedding map\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "e: V \\to R^m.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For retrieval, we usually need embeddings of longer text units: queries, sentences, paragraphs, document chunks, or full documents.  Let $T$ denote the set of all finite token sequences.  A text embedding model is a map\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f_\\theta: T \\to R^m.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For a query $q \\in T$ and a document chunk $c \\in T$, we compute\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "z_q = f_\\theta(q),\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "z_c = f_\\theta(c).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The retrieval score is then a function\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "s(q,c) = S(z_q,z_c),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $S:R^m \\times R^m \\to R$ is a similarity measure.  Common choices are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "S_{\\text{dot}}(z_q,z_c)=z_q^T z_c,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "S_{\\text{cos}}(z_q,z_c)=\\frac{z_q^T z_c}{\\|z_q\\|\\|z_c\\|},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "S_{\\text{neg-dist}}(z_q,z_c)=-\\|z_q-z_c\\|_2^2.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The top-$k$ retrieval problem is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{TopK}(q)\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\operatorname{argtopk}_{c \\in C} S(f_\\theta(q),f_\\theta(c)).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a finite optimization problem over a collection of vectors.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.4 Sparse retrieval versus dense retrieval\n",
    "\n",
    "Classical information retrieval often represents a document by a sparse vector.  If the vocabulary has size $d$, then a document $c$ may be represented as\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_c \\in R^d,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $x_{cj}$ is a count or TF-IDF weight for token $j$.  A query $q$ is represented similarly by $x_q$, and the score is often a dot product or cosine similarity.\n",
    "\n",
    "Dense retrieval instead represents the same text by\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "z_c \\in R^m,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $m$ is usually much smaller than $d$, and the coordinates are learned.  Dense embeddings are designed so that semantically related texts can be close even when they do not share exact words.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Representation | Vector dimension | Coordinates mean | Strength | Weakness |\n",
    "|---|---:|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Bag-of-words or TF-IDF | $d=|V|$ | token weights | exact lexical matching | weak semantic matching |\n",
    "| Dense embedding | $m$ | learned features | semantic matching | can miss exact constraints |\n",
    "| Hybrid retrieval | both | lexical and semantic | often robust | more complex |\n",
    "\n",
    "The mathematical difference is important.  Sparse retrieval mostly uses explicit coordinates corresponding to words.  Dense retrieval uses an embedding map learned from data.  Thus, dense retrieval transfers part of the retrieval problem into representation learning.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.5 Similarity geometry\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose all embeddings are nonzero.  Define normalized embeddings by\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\widehat z = \\frac{z}{\\|z\\|_2}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For normalized vectors,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\widehat z_q^T \\widehat z_c\n",
    "= \\cos(\\widehat z_q,\\widehat z_c).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Also,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\|\\widehat z_q - \\widehat z_c\\|_2^2\n",
    "= \\|\\widehat z_q\\|^2 + \\|\\widehat z_c\\|^2 - 2\\widehat z_q^T\\widehat z_c\n",
    "= 2 - 2\\widehat z_q^T\\widehat z_c.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, for normalized vectors, maximizing cosine similarity is equivalent to minimizing Euclidean distance:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{argmax}_c \\widehat z_q^T \\widehat z_c\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\operatorname{argmin}_c \\|\\widehat z_q-\\widehat z_c\\|_2.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is why many vector databases support both inner-product search and Euclidean nearest-neighbor search.  With normalized embeddings, these objectives are equivalent up to a monotone transformation.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-warning}\n",
    "## Normalization changes the retrieval problem\n",
    "\n",
    "Without normalization, the dot product depends on both direction and vector length:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "z_q^T z_c = \\|z_q\\|\\|z_c\\|\\cos(z_q,z_c).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus, a long vector can score highly because of its norm, not only because of semantic alignment.  Cosine similarity removes this norm effect.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.6 Retrieval as matrix multiplication\n",
    "\n",
    "Let the collection contain $n$ chunks $c_1,\\dots,c_n$.  Stack their embeddings as rows of a matrix\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Z =\n",
    "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
    "- z_{c_1}^T - \\\\\n",
    "- z_{c_2}^T - \\\\\n",
    "\\vdots \\\\\n",
    "- z_{c_n}^T -\n",
    "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "\\in R^{n \\times m}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For a query embedding $z_q \\in R^m$, dot-product scores are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "s = Z z_q \\in R^n.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The $i$th component is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "s_i = z_{c_i}^T z_q.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For cosine retrieval, first normalize each row of $Z$ and the query vector:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\widehat Z_i = \\frac{z_{c_i}}{\\|z_{c_i}\\|},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\widehat z_q = \\frac{z_q}{\\|z_q\\|}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Then\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "s = \\widehat Z \\widehat z_q.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "So dense retrieval is mathematically simple: compute an embedding, multiply by a matrix, sort the scores.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.7 A small retrieval example\n",
    "\n",
    "Consider six short chunks:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. \"students may transfer graduate credits with approval\"\n",
    "2. \"the course covers stochastic processes and Markov chains\"\n",
    "3. \"the library has study rooms and research databases\"\n",
    "4. \"machine learning uses embeddings and optimization\"\n",
    "5. \"applied mathematics students study probability and linear algebra\"\n",
    "6. \"transfer credit forms require the program director signature\"\n",
    "\n",
    "A query such as \"How do I transfer credits?\" should retrieve chunks 1 and 6.  A query such as \"What math background is needed for machine learning?\" should retrieve chunks 4 and 5.\n",
    "\n",
    "A sparse lexical system will rely on words like \"transfer\" and \"credit.\"  A dense embedding system may also learn that \"approval,\" \"form,\" and \"signature\" are related to administrative transfer rules.  The central question is how well the embedding map preserves the task-relevant notion of similarity.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.8 Python: dense retrieval from scratch with toy embeddings\n",
    "\n",
    "The following example builds a tiny semantic search engine using manually designed low-dimensional embeddings.  The goal is not to train a real embedding model; the goal is to make the retrieval geometry transparent."
   ]
