   "source": [
    "TF-IDF can be understood as a diagonal rescaling of a document-term matrix.  If $X \\in R^{n \\times d}$ is a count matrix and $D \\in R^{d \\times d}$ is a diagonal matrix of inverse-document-frequency weights, then the TF-IDF matrix is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X_{\\text{tfidf}} = X D.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Retrieval is then performed using row-normalized vectors from $X_{\\text{tfidf}}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.10 Hybrid retrieval\n",
    "\n",
    "In many applications, sparse and dense retrieval are complementary.  One simple hybrid score is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "s_{\\text{hybrid}}(q,c)\n",
    "= \\lambda s_{\\text{dense}}(q,c)\n",
    "+ (1-\\lambda)s_{\\text{sparse}}(q,c),\n",
    "\\qquad 0 \\leq \\lambda \\leq 1.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Here $\\lambda$ controls the tradeoff between semantic matching and exact-word matching.  This formula is simple but useful.  It also makes a conceptual point: retrieval design is often a choice of scoring function."
   ]
