    "## 19.11 Reranking\n",
    "\n",
    "A common retrieval pipeline has two stages:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Retriever:** quickly returns a candidate set $C_k(q)$.\n",
    "2. **Reranker:** applies a more expensive scoring function to reorder the candidates.\n",
    "\n",
    "Mathematically, the first stage solves an approximate top-$k$ problem:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C_k(q) \\approx \\operatorname{argtopk}_{c \\in C} S_1(q,c).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The reranker then solves\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{argtopr}_{c \\in C_k(q)} S_2(q,c),\n",
    "\\qquad r \\leq k.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The first score $S_1$ is usually cheap, such as vector similarity.  The second score $S_2$ may use a cross-encoder or a larger language model that jointly reads the query and candidate chunk.  This is more expensive because the representation of the pair $(q,c)$ must be computed jointly for each candidate.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.12 Chunking as a mathematical design choice\n",
    "\n",
    "Before indexing a document collection, long documents are usually split into chunks.  Let a document be a sequence\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "d = (w_1,\\dots,w_T).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "A chunking function maps $d$ to shorter subsequences:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "g(d) = (c_1,\\dots,c_M).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, fixed-length chunking with length $L$ and stride $s$ creates chunks\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "c_j = (w_{1+(j-1)s},\\dots,w_{L+(j-1)s}).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Chunking is not merely a technical preprocessing step.  It affects retrieval quality.\n",
    "\n",
    "- If chunks are too short, they may lack context.\n",
    "- If chunks are too long, they may contain irrelevant material and waste the model context window.\n",
    "- If chunks overlap too much, the index becomes redundant.\n",
    "- If chunks do not respect document structure, important definitions or assumptions may be separated.\n",
    "\n",
    "In mathematical terms, chunking determines the finite candidate set $C$ over which retrieval is performed.  A poor candidate set cannot be fixed completely by a good similarity function.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.13 RAG as conditional generation\n",
    "\n",
    "A simple RAG pipeline is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "q \\xrightarrow{\\text{embed}} z_q\n",
    "\\xrightarrow{\\text{retrieve}} c_{i_1},\\dots,c_{i_k}\n",
    "\\xrightarrow{\\text{prompt}} x\n",
    "\\xrightarrow{\\text{LM}} y.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The context-augmented prompt may be written as\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x = \\operatorname{concat}(q,c_{i_1},\\dots,c_{i_k},I),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $I$ denotes the instruction template.  The generator then samples or chooses\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y \\sim p_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid x).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This clarifies an important point: retrieval does not force the generator to be correct.  Retrieval changes the conditioning information.  The final answer still depends on the conditional distribution learned by the language model and on the decoding procedure.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.14 A probabilistic latent-document view\n",
    "\n",
    "A more formal RAG model treats retrieved documents as latent variables.  Let $C$ be the collection.  Then\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(y \\mid q)\n",
    "= \\sum_{c \\in C} p_\\theta(y \\mid q,c) p_\\eta(c \\mid q).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "In practice, summing over all $c \\in C$ is expensive, so we approximate by the top-$k$ retrieved chunks:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(y \\mid q)\n",
    "\\approx\n",
    "\\sum_{c \\in C_k(q)} p_\\theta(y \\mid q,c) p_\\eta(c \\mid q).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "If the retriever assigns scores $s(q,c)$, a soft retrieval distribution can be defined by\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p_\\eta(c \\mid q)\n",
    "= \\frac{\\exp(s(q,c)/\\tau)}{\\sum_{c' \\in C} \\exp(s(q,c')/\\tau)}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The temperature $\\tau$ controls concentration.  A small $\\tau$ concentrates probability on the top chunks.  A large $\\tau$ spreads probability across many chunks.\n",
    "\n",
    "This formulation makes retrieval uncertainty explicit.  If several chunks have similar scores, then the system should be less confident about which evidence is most relevant.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.15 Evaluating retrieval\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose for a query $q$ we know a set of relevant documents $R(q) \\subseteq C$.  If a retriever returns an ordered list\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "L_k(q) = (c_{i_1},\\dots,c_{i_k}),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "then precision at $k$ is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{Precision@}k(q)\n",
    "= \\frac{|L_k(q) \\cap R(q)|}{k}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Recall at $k$ is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{Recall@}k(q)\n",
    "= \\frac{|L_k(q) \\cap R(q)|}{|R(q)|}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The reciprocal rank is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{RR}(q)\n",
    "= \\frac{1}{\\min\\{j:c_{i_j}\\in R(q)\\}},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "if a relevant result appears, and $0$ otherwise.  Mean reciprocal rank averages this quantity over queries.\n",
    "\n",
    "For graded relevance labels $rel_j$, discounted cumulative gain is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{DCG@}k\n",
    "= \\sum_{j=1}^k \\frac{2^{rel_j}-1}{\\log_2(j+1)}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Normalized DCG divides by the best possible DCG:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{nDCG@}k\n",
    "= \\frac{\\operatorname{DCG@}k}{\\operatorname{IDCG@}k}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "These metrics measure retrieval, not necessarily final answer quality.  A RAG system can retrieve the right document and still generate a poor answer; it can also retrieve incomplete evidence and produce an answer that sounds plausible but is unsupported.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.16 Python: retrieval metrics"
