    "## 19.17 Approximate nearest-neighbor search\n",
    "\n",
    "Exact retrieval by matrix multiplication requires computing $Zz_q$ over all $n$ stored vectors.  This costs $O(nm)$ per query.  For small $n$, this is fine.  For millions or billions of chunks, exact search may be too expensive.\n",
    "\n",
    "Approximate nearest-neighbor methods build an index that returns a candidate set quickly.  The mathematical tradeoff is between:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **speed:** how fast queries are answered;\n",
    "- **memory:** how large the index is;\n",
    "- **recall:** how often the true nearest neighbors are returned;\n",
    "- **update cost:** how hard it is to add or remove vectors.\n",
    "\n",
    "At a high level, many methods use one of the following ideas.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Partitioning\n",
    "\n",
    "Partition the vector space into regions.  Search only regions near the query.  This reduces the number of candidate vectors.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Graph search\n",
    "\n",
    "Build a graph whose vertices are stored vectors and whose edges connect nearby vectors.  Starting from an entry point, greedily move through the graph toward vectors closer to the query.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Quantization\n",
    "\n",
    "Approximate vectors by compressed codes.  Search is faster and uses less memory, but distances are approximate.\n",
    "\n",
    "For this book, the key point is that vector indexing is an algorithmic approximation to the mathematical top-$k$ problem.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.18 Worked calculation: cosine retrieval\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose a query and three chunk embeddings are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "z_q = (1,1),\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "z_1=(2,2),\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "z_2=(2,0),\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "z_3=(0,3).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Dot-product scores are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "z_1^Tz_q=4,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "z_2^Tz_q=2,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "z_3^Tz_q=3.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus dot-product retrieval ranks $z_1,z_3,z_2$.  Cosine scores are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\cos(z_q,z_1)=1,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\cos(z_q,z_2)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt 2},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\cos(z_q,z_3)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt 2}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Under cosine similarity, $z_2$ and $z_3$ tie.  The difference occurs because dot product rewards the longer norm of $z_3$.\n",
