    "## 19.21 Error modes in RAG\n",
    "\n",
    "RAG systems can fail for mathematically distinct reasons.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Retrieval failure\n",
    "\n",
    "The relevant chunk is not retrieved:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "R(q) \\cap L_k(q) = \\varnothing.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This may happen because the embedding model fails to place the query near the right document, because the relevant document was not indexed, or because the chunking strategy split the answer from its context.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Ranking failure\n",
    "\n",
    "A relevant chunk is retrieved, but ranked too low to be included in the final context.  This is a recall and ranking problem.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Context overload\n",
    "\n",
    "Too many chunks are provided.  The answer-relevant evidence may be present, but the generator may attend to the wrong material or combine incompatible chunks.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Generation failure\n",
    "\n",
    "The correct evidence is present, but the language model misreads it, ignores it, or produces unsupported text.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Citation failure\n",
    "\n",
    "The answer may be mostly correct but cite the wrong chunk.  This is a mismatch between answer generation and evidence attribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note}\n",
    "## A useful diagnostic separation\n",
    "\n",
    "When debugging a RAG system, separate three questions:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Did retrieval find the right evidence?\n",
    "2. Did the prompt place the evidence in a usable form?\n",
    "3. Did the generator faithfully use the evidence?\n",
    "\n",
    "These are different mathematical and engineering problems.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.22 RAG for academic advising and course resources\n",
    "\n",
    "A university resource chatbot is a natural RAG application.  The document collection may include:\n",
    "\n",
    "- program requirements;\n",
    "- course descriptions;\n",
    "- transfer-credit policies;\n",
    "- advising FAQs;\n",
    "- academic calendar pages;\n",
    "- graduate catalog entries;\n",
    "- official forms and procedures.\n",
    "\n",
    "For such a system, mathematical care matters.  The answer should be grounded in retrieved official sources, and the system should communicate uncertainty when retrieval is weak.\n",
    "\n",
    "A minimal retrieval-aware answer generator should keep track of:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{query},\\quad\n",
    "\\text{retrieved chunks},\\quad\n",
    "\\text{similarity scores},\\quad\n",
    "\\text{source metadata},\\quad\n",
    "\\text{answer}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The source metadata are essential.  Without them, the system may generate fluent but unverifiable responses.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.23 AI-assisted learning activities\n",
    "\n",
    "Use an AI assistant as a mathematical critic, not only as a code generator.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Activity 1: explain the scoring geometry\n",
    "\n",
    "Ask an AI assistant:\n",
    "\n",
    "> In dense retrieval, explain the difference between dot-product similarity and cosine similarity.  Give a two-dimensional example where they rank documents differently.\n",
    "\n",
    "Then verify the example by hand.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Activity 2: audit a RAG pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "Give the assistant a proposed RAG design and ask:\n",
    "\n",
    "> Identify possible retrieval failure modes, ranking failure modes, and generation failure modes.  For each one, suggest a quantitative diagnostic.\n",
    "\n",
    "Check whether the suggested metrics actually correspond to the failure modes.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Activity 3: create adversarial queries\n",
    "\n",
    "Ask the assistant to produce queries that are likely to confuse a retriever:\n",
    "\n",
    "- queries with synonyms;\n",
    "- queries with exact codes or course numbers;\n",
    "- queries requiring negation;\n",
    "- queries requiring date-sensitive information;\n",
    "- queries requiring multiple documents.\n",
    "\n",
    "Then test whether dense, sparse, or hybrid retrieval handles each case better.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 19.24 Common mistakes\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Treating similarity scores as probabilities.**  A cosine score is not automatically a calibrated probability of relevance.\n",
    "2. **Ignoring chunking.**  Retrieval quality depends on the candidate units.\n",
    "3. **Using only dense retrieval.**  Exact keywords, identifiers, and policy names often require lexical matching.\n",
    "4. **Assuming retrieved means true.**  Retrieved text may be outdated, irrelevant, or misinterpreted.\n",
    "5. **Evaluating only final answers.**  Retrieval metrics and generation metrics should be separated.\n",
    "6. **Overloading the context window.**  More retrieved text is not always better.\n",
    "\n",
